  "explanation": {
    "short": "A vowel before one consonant, or at the end of a word, is long (ha, her, du). A vowel before a double consonant is short (takk, vann, kaffe), so doubling a consonant shortens the vowel before it. u is a rounded \"ee\": say \"ee\" and round your lips. A final -e (kaffe) is a weak \"uh\".",
    "more": "This rule is a very good guide, but a few very common words are exceptions (til, vil, kom): learn those as they come. \"tak\" (roof) and \"takk\" (thanks) differ only in the length of the a."
  },
